Subject: main.c: issue TIOCSCTTY on substrate to acquire the pty ctty

Substrate's kernel deliberately does NOT auto-acquire a controlling
terminal when a session leader opens a pty slave: tty_attach_first_opener
excludes ptyslave/ptmx so that luit's parent cannot steal the pair before
the setsid'd child claims it.  A PTY controlling terminal is therefore
established ONLY by an explicit TIOCSCTTY.

xterm's success-path TIOCSCTTY (issued right after opening the slave, in
the child that has already setsid'd) is gated behind
((__GLIBC__ && __FreeBSD_kernel__) || __GNU__) && TIOCSCTTY -- none of
which substrate matches -- so the shell was left with no controlling
terminal: `tty` still worked (it reads the fd) but `ps` reported the tty
as '?' and job control was half-broken.  The only TIOCSCTTY xterm reached
on substrate was the one in the open-failure path, too late to help.

Add __substrate__ (a substrate-gcc built-in) to the guard.

--- a/main.c
+++ b/main.c
@@ -4556,7 +4556,11 @@
 			/* make /dev/tty work */
 			ioctl(ttyfd, TCSETCTTY, 0);
 #endif
-#if ((defined(__GLIBC__) && defined(__FreeBSD_kernel__)) || defined(__GNU__)) && defined(TIOCSCTTY)
-			/* make /dev/tty work */
+#if ((defined(__GLIBC__) && defined(__FreeBSD_kernel__)) || defined(__GNU__) || defined(__substrate__)) && defined(TIOCSCTTY)
+			/* make /dev/tty work.  Substrate: PTYs never auto-acquire
+			 * a controlling terminal on open (kernel excludes ptyslave
+			 * from tty_attach_first_opener), so the setsid'd child must
+			 * issue TIOCSCTTY here in the success path or the shell runs
+			 * with no ctty (ps shows tty '?', job control half-broken). */
 			ioctl(ttyfd, TIOCSCTTY, 0);
 #endif
